{id:"e-suwayqatayn",titleEn:"Dhul-Suwayqatayn",titleAr:"ذو السويقتين",role:"The demolisher of the Kaaba",summary:"After the last believer is taken, a thin-legged man from Abyssinia dismantles the House stone by stone.",pattern:"pattern-beast",
details:`The House that {{n:11|Ibrahim raised}}, that {{n:25|the birds defended}}, that {{n:41|the Opening purified}}, has one more appointment in its file, and the sound tradition does not soften it: "Dhul-Suwayqatayn, the man of two thin legs, from Abyssinia, will demolish the Kaaba" (Bukhari 1591, Muslim 2909). The Prophet ﷺ described the scene with terrible precision: "as if I am looking at him: black, bandy-legged, dismantling it stone by stone" (Bukhari 1595).

The sequencing is the theology. This does not happen while one worshipper remains: it follows {{n:55|the gentle wind}} that harvests every believing soul, in the age when no one on earth says Allah, Allah. The hadith corpus adds the closures around it: the Hajj and Umrah will have been performed to the end of their appointed era, and in the hour of the worst of people the House's protection file, held since the Elephant, is allowed to lapse, because a sanctuary's sanctity lived in the ummah that honored it. The Codex keeps this darkest seal deliberately: buildings, even that building, were never the point; the direction of prayer was a means, and when no one prays, stones return to being stones. The believer reading this will never see it; that is precisely the mercy of the sequence.`,
facts:[{label:"Description",value:"Thin-legged, from Abyssinia"},{label:"Act",value:"Stone by stone"},{label:"Timing",value:"After the wind; no believers remain"},{label:"Seen by him ﷺ",value:"“As if I am looking at him”"}],
hadith:[{text:"Dhul-Suwayqatayn from Abyssinia will demolish the Kaaba… as if I am looking at him, dismantling it stone by stone.",source:"Bukhari 1591, 1595 · Muslim 2909"}]}
